data.content:
Here's the full migration. Your code has three patterns to replace:

| Pattern Found | Replacement |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` in `Stop()` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` + cleanup | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested` to decide whether to continue |

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        var token = _cts.Token;
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token so blocking calls can wake up
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work — migrated from ThreadAbortException catch
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed. Loop condition will exit naturally.
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait up to 2 seconds for the thread to finish
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(2000));
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

## Key Changes & Pitfalls

**1. `GetNextItem()` must accept and honor the token.**
This is the most critical pitfall. If it blocks (e.g., `Thread.Sleep`, `queue.Take()`), cancellation will stall until it returns. Fix:

```csharp
// Before — blocks indefinitely, ignores cancellation
var item = queue.Take();

// After — wakes immediately on cancel, throws OperationCanceledException
var item = queue.Take(token);
```

**2. `Thread.ResetAbort()` has no equivalent — and you don't need one.**
The old code used `ResetAbort` to keep the thread alive after abort. With cooperative cancellation, the loop simply checks `token.IsCancellationRequested` at the top and exits cleanly.

**3. `Thread.Abort()` → `cts.Cancel()` is *not* immediate.**
`Thread.Abort` injected an exception anywhere in the thread. `Cancel()` only takes effect at the next checkpoint (`ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` or a token-aware API). If `ProcessItem` is long-running, add checkpoints:

```csharp
private void ProcessItem(Item item, CancellationToken token)
{
    Step1(item);
    token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
    Step2(item);
    token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
    Step3(item);
}
```

**4. `Thread.Sleep` → `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne`.**
If any code in the call chain uses `Thread.Sleep`, replace it:

```csharp
// Before — ignores cancellation
Thread.Sleep(5000);

// After — wakes on cancel
token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(5000);
```

**5. Dispose `CancellationTokenSource`.**
It's `IDisposable` — leaking it causes timer/callback leaks. That's why the class now implements `IDisposable`.

**6. Use `Join` instead of `Sleep` + `IsAlive`.**
The original `Stop()` used `Thread.Sleep(2000)` then checked `IsAlive`. `Thread.Join(timeout)` does this correctly in one call and is valid in .NET 8.
